  "summary": "Norwegian whitework of satin blocks and cut-and-woven windows — snowflake geometry in white on white.",
  "description": "Hardanger embroidery builds counted satin-stitch blocks of Kloster blocks, then cuts away fabric threads beneath and weaves the exposed grid into picots, wheels and dove's-eye windows — all in white linen thread on white cloth. Farm women of the Hardanger fjord dressed aprons, caps and church linens in this disciplined geometry, likely descended from Middle Eastern drawnwork via Renaissance trade routes. Emigration carried it to America where \"Hardanger\" became a beloved society craft; modern stitchers keep the grid grammar alive worldwide.",
  "culturalContext": "In the period between 1650-1850 Hardangersom (meaning: work from Hardanger area) flourished in Norway. Flax was grown, carded, spun and woven into white fabric and thread which was used to make and decorate traditional Norwegian costume items called bunads (national costumes) as well as other items of clothing and household linens such as mats, curtains and bedspreads.\n\nHardanger is used on the bunad from the Hardanger region of Norway. It is usually found on the cuffs, collar and front yoke of the shirt, on the apron, and sometimes on a band around the pleated headdress.",
